<div class="meta">Lesson 0009 · Multiplayer networking · from feat(net): NetServer and NetClient</div>
<h1>Server-Authoritative Replication and the Thread Contract</h1>
<p>How four players share one world without sharing one crash.</p>

<h2>Who owns the truth?</h2>
<p>In a networked game, some machine must be the referee. Two players both think
they reached the hot-dog cart first — whose claim wins? This game answers the way
most multiplayer games do: <strong>the host is authoritative</strong>. The host's
<code>World</code>, its NPC positions, moods, and conversation histories are the only
real ones. Clients hold a <em>replica</em>: a copy that's a few dozen milliseconds
stale, refreshed ~12 times a second by <code>WorldSnapshot</code> broadcasts.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Direction</th><th>Message</th><th>Content</th></tr>
<tr><td>client → host</td><td><code>PlayerInput</code></td><td>my position + facing this frame</td></tr>
<tr><td>host → all</td><td><code>WorldSnapshot</code></td><td>every player pose + every NPC pose/mood/behavior</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Joined clients <em>stop simulating entirely</em> — <code>main.cpp</code> skips
<code>npc.update()</code> when joined and renders straight from the last snapshot. A replica
that also simulates drifts from the authority; then you need reconciliation
machinery. Not simulating is the simplest correct thing.</p>

<h2>The three server threads</h2>
<pre><code>accept thread    one blocking accept() loop; spawns a reader per connection
reader threads   one per client: recv → FrameAssembler → decodeMessage → apply
tick thread      every 1/12 s: build snapshot, broadcast, reap dead connections</code></pre>
<p>Each blocking concern gets its own thread so nothing can starve anything else: a
client that stalls mid-handshake only stalls <em>its own</em> reader. Shutdown is the
subtle part — a blocked <code>recv()</code> doesn't return just because you set a flag. The
trick used throughout <code>NetServer</code>/<code>NetClient</code>:
<code>shutdown(fd, SHUT_RDWR)</code> forces the blocked call to return, <em>then</em>
<code>join()</code> the thread, <em>then</em> <code>close(fd)</code>. Closing before joining risks the
kernel recycling the fd number while the old thread still uses it.</p>

<h2>The thread contract (the load-bearing decision)</h2>
<div class="callout good"><strong>No network thread ever touches <code>World</code>, <code>Npc</code>, or
<code>LlmClient</code>.</strong> Game state crosses the boundary only as values through
mutex-guarded queues, pumped by the host's main loop.</div>
<pre><code>// host main loop, once per frame:
netServer-&gt;setHostPose(camera.position, camera.yawDeg);   // value in
netServer-&gt;publishNpcPoses(posesCopiedFromWorld);          // values in
chatRouter-&gt;update();   // drains drainChatEvents() — events out</code></pre>
<p>Why so strict? The alternative is the tick thread reading
<code>world.npcs()[i].position()</code> while the render loop writes it — a data race, which
in C++ is undefined behavior even when it "mostly works". Locking World instead
would leak mutexes into every system that touches NPCs, including solo play. The
publish/drain contract keeps <em>all</em> game logic single-threaded — exactly as it was
before multiplayer existed — which is why the solo code path didn't change.</p>
<p>This is the same pattern <code>LlmClient</code> already used: its worker thread never
touches NPCs either; the main loop drains <code>drainDeltas()</code>/<code>drainReplies()</code>.
Multiplayer just applied the house pattern to a second subsystem.</p>

<h2>The handshake: reject early, reject loudly</h2>
<ol>
<li>Client connects and sends <code>JoinRequest {version, name, code}</code>.</li>
<li>Server checks, in order: <strong>protocol version</strong> (mismatched builds get told to
update, not left to desync), <strong>join code</strong>, <strong>capacity</strong> (lowest free id
1..3; none free → "session full").</li>
<li><code>Welcome {accepted, playerId}</code> or <code>Welcome {accepted:false, reason}</code>.</li>
</ol>
<div class="callout">The version check is the cheapest insurance in the protocol: one int
compared once per join, versus hours of debugging why your friend's older build
renders everyone at the origin.</div>

<h2>Where the ids live</h2>
<p>The host is always player 0. Remote ids (1-3) are assigned at handshake and
<em>reused</em> after a disconnect — the session-full test proves a slot frees up when
someone leaves. NPCs are identified by roster index, which is safe because this
codebase never deletes NPCs (an existing invariant the wire protocol now depends
on).</p>
